  "onboardingMeasurementTipNote": "Enter the dimensions in meters and the app automatically calculates the area in m².",
  "faqBehangenQ1": "How long does wallpapering take on average?",
  "faqBehangenA1": "Wallpapering a room takes on average half to a full day, depending on the size of the room and the number of corners and niches.",
  "faqBehangenQ2": "How many wallpaper rolls do I need?",
  "faqBehangenA2": "Calculate the room perimeter (in meters) multiplied by the height. A standard roll covers about 5 m². Don't forget to order 10% extra for cutting loss.",
  "faqBehangenQ3": "What tools do I need?",
  "faqBehangenA3": "For wallpapering you need: a wallpaper table, wallpaper brush, paste roller, tape measure, spirit level, pencil, scissors or wallpaper knife, and sponges to wipe away excess paste.",
  "faqBehangenQ4": "Which wallpaper paste should I use?",
  "faqBehangenA4": "This depends on the type of wallpaper. For non-woven wallpaper, use a special non-woven paste; for regular wallpaper, use a universal paste. Always check the packaging.",
  "faqBehangenQ5": "How do I prepare the wall?",
  "faqBehangenA5": "Remove old wallpaper thoroughly and clean the wall. Let the wall dry completely. Then apply a coat of wallpaper primer for better adhesion.",
  "faqBehangenQ6": "How do I start in the room?",
  "faqBehangenA6": "Always start next to a window or door. Draw a vertical line with a spirit level as a starting point to prevent crooked strips.",
  "faqBehangenQ7": "How do I handle corners correctly?",
  "faqBehangenA7": "For inner corners, let the strip run 1–2 cm around the corner. For outer corners, fold the strip at least 2 cm around the corner.",
  "faqBehangenQ8": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqBehangenA8": "Yes, with the right preparation wallpapering is perfectly manageable on your own. Start with a simple plain wallpaper without large patterns.",
  "faqBehangenQ9": "How do I prevent air bubbles?",
  "faqBehangenA9": "Smooth the wallpaper immediately after applying, from top to bottom, with a wallpaper brush. Work any bubbles toward the edges.",
  "faqBehangenQ10": "How long to wait before using the room?",
  "faqBehangenA10": "Let the wallpaper dry for at least 24 hours. Ensure adequate ventilation but avoid direct drafts.",
  "faqBehangenQ11": "What are common wallpapering mistakes?",
  "faqBehangenA11": "The most common mistakes are: not properly preparing the wall, making the wallpaper too wet, and not smoothing out air bubbles.",
  "faqBehangenQ12": "How much extra wallpaper should I order?",
  "faqBehangenA12": "Always order 10–15% extra. With patterns you need to allow extra for the pattern repeat.",
  "faqBehangenQ13": "What do I do if the wallpaper comes loose?",
  "faqBehangenA13": "Press the loose edge up, apply fresh paste with a small brush and wipe away excess paste with a damp sponge.",
  "faqBehangenQ14": "Which DIY store do you recommend?",
  "faqBehangenA14": "For DIY homeowners, we recommend Gamma and Hornbach (or Home Depot and Lowe's) for a wide range of wallpaper. For professional contractors, trade suppliers like Bouwmaat and Jongeneel offer commercial-grade materials.",
  "faqBehangenQ15": "Do I need to remove old paint before wallpapering?",
  "faqBehangenA15": "Not always. Make sure the paint layer is firmly attached, clean and grease-free. Loose paint must be removed.",
  "faqBehangenQ16": "Can I wallpaper over existing wallpaper?",
  "faqBehangenA16": "This is usually not recommended. Remove old wallpaper for the best result and good adhesion of the new wallpaper.",
  "faqBehangenQ17": "What temperature is ideal for wallpapering?",
  "faqBehangenA17": "The ideal room temperature is between 18°C and 22°C. Avoid extreme cold, heat and drafts during drying.",
  "faqBehangenQ18": "How do I cut wallpaper neatly?",
  "faqBehangenA18": "Use a sharp wallpaper knife and a ruler or spatula as a guide. Replace the knife regularly for clean cuts.",
  "faqBehangenQ19": "How do I clean wallpaper?",
  "faqBehangenA19": "Washable wallpaper can be carefully cleaned with a damp cloth. Always check the manufacturer's care instructions first.",
  "faqBehangenQ20": "How long does wallpaper last on average?",
  "faqBehangenA20": "Quality wallpaper can last 10 to 15 years with normal use, depending on the room and maintenance conditions.",
  "faqPainterQ1": "How long does painting take on average?",
  "faqPainterA1": "Painting an average room (walls + ceiling) takes 4–8 hours including preparation. Accounting for drying time between coats it can take 1–2 days.",
  "faqPainterQ2": "How much paint do I need?",
  "faqPainterA2": "One litre of paint covers on average 10–12 m² per coat. Measure the surface and allow for 2 coats. Order 10% extra as a reserve.",
  "faqPainterQ3": "What tools do I need?",
  "faqPainterA3": "You need: paint roller(s), brush(es), paint tray, masking tape, sandpaper, dust sheets and possibly a ladder.",
  "faqPainterQ4": "How do I prepare the wall?",
  "faqPainterA4": "Fill any cracks and holes, sand rough areas, remove dust and apply a coat of primer on new or very absorbent walls.",
  "faqPainterQ5": "How long should I wait between coats?",
  "faqPainterA5": "Wait at least 2–4 hours between coats (see drying time on the packaging). Full curing takes 24–48 hours.",
  "faqPainterQ6": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqPainterA6": "Yes, painting is perfectly manageable on your own. Take your time with proper preparation and masking and the result will be excellent.",
  "faqPainterQ7": "Which paint should I choose for interior walls?",
  "faqPainterA7": "Choose water-based wall paint for interior walls. Pay attention to the washability category (1–5 stars). For wet rooms, choose bathroom paint.",
  "faqPainterQ8": "What are common painting mistakes?",
  "faqPainterA8": "Applying coats that are too thin, insufficient drying between coats, poor masking and not cleaning the surface are the most common mistakes.",
  "faqPainterQ9": "Do I need to use primer?",
  "faqPainterA9": "Primer improves adhesion and ensures a more even finish, especially on new or repaired surfaces.",
  "faqPainterQ10": "Can I paint over old paint?",
  "faqPainterA10": "Yes, if the old paint is in good condition. Clean and lightly sand the surface first.",
  "faqPainterQ11": "What temperature is best for painting?",
  "faqPainterA11": "Most paints perform best between 10°C and 25°C.",
  "faqPainterQ12": "How do I avoid roller marks?",
  "faqPainterA12": "Use even pressure and maintain a wet edge while painting.",
  "faqPainterQ13": "Should I remove furniture?",
  "faqPainterA13": "Move furniture away from walls and cover it with protective sheets.",
  "faqPainterQ14": "How do I clean paint brushes?",
  "faqPainterA14": "Water-based paint can be cleaned with water. Solvent-based paint requires paint thinner.",
  "faqPainterQ15": "Can I paint in humid conditions?",
  "faqPainterA15": "High humidity can slow drying and affect the finish. Ventilate the room well.",
  "faqPainterQ16": "How many coats are recommended?",
  "faqPainterA16": "Two coats provide the best coverage and durability.",
  "faqPainterQ17": "What finish should I choose?",
  "faqPainterA17": "Matt hides imperfections, satin is durable, and gloss is highly washable.",
  "faqPainterQ18": "How long does paint last?",
  "faqPainterA18": "Unopened paint can last several years when stored properly.",
  "faqPainterQ19": "Can I paint ceilings first?",
  "faqPainterA19": "Yes, always paint ceilings before walls to avoid splatters.",
  "faqPainterQ20": "How do I calculate painting costs?",
  "faqPainterA20": "Multiply wall area by paint coverage and material costs.",
  "faqMuurSauzenQ1": "Which latex should I use in a bathroom or kitchen?",
  "faqMuurSauzenA1": "Use moisture-resistant latex in a bathroom or kitchen. This is more resistant to moisture and mildew. Regular latex can start to peel or develop mildew more quickly in humid spaces.",
  "faqMuurSauzenQ2": "Should I degrease the wall first?",
  "faqMuurSauzenA2": "Yes, for greasy or dirty spots (e.g. in a kitchen), first clean with an all-purpose cleaner/degreaser — wear gloves — then allow to dry before painting.",
  "faqMuurSauzenQ3": "When should I use primer?",
  "faqMuurSauzenA3": "Use primer on an absorbent, new or heavily repaired surface, so the paint covers evenly and is less likely to stain.",
  "faqMuurSauzenQ4": "How do I fill holes and cracks before painting?",
  "faqMuurSauzenA4": "Fill holes and cracks with wall filler or plaster, allow to dry, and sand smooth. Seal small gaps with acrylic sealant. This prevents imperfections remaining visible through the paint.",
  "faqMuurSauzenQ5": "How many coats are needed?",
  "faqMuurSauzenA5": "Allow for at least two coats for an even result; if changing colour (e.g. from dark to light) a third coat may be required.",
  "faqMuurSauzenQ6": "Should I sand before painting?",
  "faqMuurSauzenA6": "Light sanding is part of preparation, especially on an uneven or glossy surface, so the paint adheres better. Wear a dust mask while doing this. For larger areas, a sanding machine can speed up the work.",
  "faqMuurSauzenQ7": "How do I calculate how many litres of paint I need?",
  "faqMuurSauzenA7": "The app calculates this automatically based on your entered m², the number of coats, and the coverage of the selected product.",
  "faqMuurSauzenQ8": "Can I use a brush instead of a roller?",
  "faqMuurSauzenA8": "For large surfaces, a roller is faster and more even; use a brush for corners, edges and near the baseboard.",
  "faqMuurSauzenQ9": "Should I let the paint dry between coats?",
  "faqMuurSauzenA9": "Yes, always follow the drying and recoating time on the paint packaging.",
  "faqMuurSauzenQ10": "Can I paint a wall without primer?",
  "faqMuurSauzenA10": "On a well-bonding, non-absorbent surface it is sometimes possible, but primer improves adhesion and the result. Follow the product advice.",
  "faqPlafondSauzenQ1": "Which latex should I use in a bathroom or kitchen?",
  "faqPlafondSauzenA1": "Use moisture-resistant latex in a bathroom or kitchen. This is more resistant to moisture and mildew. Regular latex can start to peel or develop mildew more quickly in humid spaces.",
  "faqPlafondSauzenQ2": "Do I need an extension pole?",
  "faqPlafondSauzenA2": "An extension pole on the roller is recommended so you can reach the ceiling without a ladder or with less ladder work.",
  "faqPlafondSauzenQ3": "Should I cover furniture and the floor?",
  "faqPlafondSauzenA3": "Yes, cover the floor and furniture with dust sheets; ceiling work splashes more than wall work.",
  "faqPlafondSauzenQ4": "How do I fill holes and cracks before painting?",
  "faqPlafondSauzenA4": "Fill holes and cracks with wall filler or plaster, allow to dry, and sand smooth. This prevents imperfections remaining visible through the paint.",
  "faqPlafondSauzenQ5": "How many coats are needed?",
  "faqPlafondSauzenA5": "Here too, allow for at least two coats for an even, opaque finish.",
  "faqPlafondSauzenQ6": "Which roller thickness is suitable for a ceiling?",
  "faqPlafondSauzenA6": "A roller with short to medium pile works well on a smooth ceiling; for a rough or textured ceiling, choose a roller with longer pile.",
  "faqPlafondSauzenQ7": "How do I prevent splashes and drips?",
  "faqPlafondSauzenA7": "Work with a properly rolled-out roller (not too much paint) and roll in calm, overlapping passes.",
  "faqPlafondSauzenQ8": "Should I prime first?",
  "faqPlafondSauzenA8": "Yes, for a new or absorbent surface; for an already painted ceiling in good condition this is usually not necessary.",
  "faqPlafondSauzenQ9": "What if there are stains, for example from water or nicotine?",
  "faqPlafondSauzenA9": "Treat these first with a suitable stain-blocking primer, otherwise they may show through again after painting.",
  "faqPlafondSauzenQ10": "Should I always paint a ceiling twice?",
  "faqPlafondSauzenA10": "For an even result, two coats are almost always needed. For a heavily absorbent or dark surface, an extra coat may be required.",
  "faqBehangenDoc4Q1": "Non-woven or paper wallpaper, what is the difference?",
  "faqBehangenDoc4A1": "With non-woven wallpaper the adhesive goes on the wall and you hang the wallpaper dry; with paper wallpaper the adhesive goes on the wallpaper itself, which then needs to soak for a while.",
  "faqBehangenDoc4Q2": "Should I level the wall first?",
  "faqBehangenDoc4A2": "Yes, the wall must be flat and clean before wallpapering. Repair holes and cracks with wall filler or levelling compound, allow to dry, and sand smooth.",
  "faqBehangenDoc4Q3": "Do I need a wallpaper table?",
  "faqBehangenDoc4A3": "Only for traditional paper wallpaper, to paste and allow the wallpaper to soak. A wallpaper table is not needed for non-woven wallpaper.",
  "faqBehangenDoc4Q4": "Which roll width should I choose?",
  "faqBehangenDoc4A4": "Non-woven wallpaper is available in 53 cm and 70 cm widths. Choose based on wall height and pattern. Calculate using the actual roll width.",
  "faqBehangenDoc4Q5": "What is pattern repeat waste?",
  "faqBehangenDoc4A5": "With patterned wallpaper you must align each strip with the pattern, which means you need to trim pieces. Allow for approximately 10% extra material.",
  "faqBehangenDoc4Q6": "Which adhesive should I use?",
  "faqBehangenDoc4A6": "It depends on the wallpaper type and weight; follow the adhesive advice on the wallpaper roll or the adhesive packaging. Wear gloves during prolonged contact with adhesive.",
  "faqBehangenDoc4Q7": "How do I deal with corners and electrical outlets?",
  "faqBehangenDoc4A7": "For electrical outlets and switches, always turn off the power first, and work in corners with smaller, overlapping strips for a tight join.",
  "faqBehangenDoc4Q8": "What should I do about drafts while drying?",
  "faqBehangenDoc4A8": "Avoid strong drafts (open windows/doors) during drying; this can cause seams to lift or come loose.",
  "faqBehangenDoc4Q9": "Can I simply wallpaper over old wallpaper?",
  "faqBehangenDoc4A9": "This is not recommended; when removing suitable old wallpaper use a wallpaper steamer if applicable, and start with a clean, flat surface.",
  "faqBehangenDoc4Q10": "How do I prevent air bubbles under the wallpaper?",
  "faqBehangenDoc4A10": "Work in small sections, smooth the wallpaper well with a wallpaper brush or seam roller, and ensure the adhesive is applied evenly.",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenQ1": "Should I degrease first?",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenA1": "Yes, degreasing is the first step, especially for frames and doors that are frequently touched. Wear gloves while doing this.",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenQ2": "When should I use primer?",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenA2": "Use primer on bare wood, or when going from a dark to a light colour.",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenQ3": "How do I recognize a lead-containing old paint layer?",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenA3": "If uncertain about old woodwork: do not sand with a machine yourself while dry, but have the layer assessed or tested first. When working with old paint layers always wear a dust mask (FFP2/FFP3) and gloves in any case.",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenQ4": "How many finish coats are needed?",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenA4": "Allow for at least two finish coats after the primer for a durable result.",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenQ5": "Should I sand between coats?",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenA5": "Light intermediate sanding provides a smoother, more even final coat; follow the product advice. For larger areas, a sanding machine can speed up the work.",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenQ6": "Can I use paint and varnish interchangeably?",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenA6": "No, use one paint system from start to finish; mixing different systems can cause adhesion problems.",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenQ7": "How do I repair holes with wood filler?",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenA7": "Fill the hole or damage with wood filler, allow to dry according to the product instructions, then sand smooth before priming.",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenQ8": "Must I take the door off its hinges to paint?",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenA8": "It is not always necessary, but it can make the work easier. In any case, ensure a stable surface and sufficient space.",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenQ9": "Can I paint outside in rain or cold?",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenA9": "No, paint outside in dry weather and a temperature suitable for the paint system; moisture and cold affect drying and adhesion.",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenQ10": "How long should I wait before using the door again?",
  "faqKozijnSchilderenA10": "Follow the drying time of the final finish coat on the packaging before using the door intensively again.",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenQ1": "Must the radiator be cold?",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenA1": "Yes, allow the radiator to cool down completely and turn off the heating before starting.",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenQ2": "Should I degrease before painting?",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenA2": "Yes, degrease the radiator first, especially a surface that is often warm — grease and dust stick there more quickly. Wear gloves while doing this.",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenQ3": "What should I do about rust?",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenA3": "Treat rust spots before painting, for example with a suitable rust converter or rust inhibitor. Wear gloves while doing this and, when sanding rust, a dust mask.",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenQ4": "Which primer should I use?",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenA4": "Use a metal primer that matches the chosen paint system, especially on bare or rusted areas.",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenQ5": "Which paint is suitable for a radiator?",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenA5": "Use radiator paint; it is heat-resistant and yellows less quickly than ordinary wall paint or wood varnish.",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenQ6": "How many coats are needed?",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenA6": "Usually one coat of primer (where needed) and one to two coats of radiator paint are sufficient; follow the product advice.",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenQ7": "How long must the paint dry before I turn the heating back on?",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenA7": "Follow the drying time of the radiator paint as stated on the packaging before using the heating again.",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenQ8": "Can I paint the radiator without dismantling it?",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenA8": "Yes, that is possible with a radiator roller and brush behind the pipes; dismantling is usually not necessary.",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenQ9": "Should I cover the wall behind it?",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenA9": "Yes, cover the wall and floor behind and beneath the radiator with dust sheets and masking tape.",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenQ10": "Which brush should I use for the radiator?",
  "faqRadiatorSchilderenA10": "A radiator roller for the large surfaces and a narrow brush for the hard-to-reach areas behind the pipes.",
  "faqStopcontactQ1": "Do I need to turn off the power before I start?",
  "faqStopcontactA1": "Yes, always switch off the relevant circuit at the fuse box and check with a voltage tester that the circuit is truly de-energized before you begin.",
  "faqStopcontactQ2": "Which tools do I need for installing a power outlet?",
  "faqStopcontactA2": "You need a screwdriver set, wire strippers, side cutters, voltage tester, spirit level, pencil and possibly a hammer drill (for fixing holes).",
  "faqStopcontactQ3": "How do I connect the wires correctly?",
  "faqStopcontactA3": "Follow the color coding: brown (live), blue (neutral) and yellow-green (earth). Check your local regulations (e.g. NEN 1010) and the outlet instructions.",
  "faqStopcontactQ4": "Can I replace an outlet myself?",
  "faqStopcontactA4": "Replacing an existing outlet is relatively simple if you have basic electrical knowledge and follow safety rules. For new installations, always work to the NEN 1010 standard.",
  "faqStopcontactQ5": "How deep should the flush-mounting box be?",
  "faqStopcontactA5": "A standard flush-mounting box for an outlet is about 40–50 mm deep. Use a dry-wall saw or masonry bit to cut the recess.",
  "faqStopcontactQ6": "What should I do if there is no earth wire?",
  "faqStopcontactA6": "In older installations there may be no earth wire. For safety, always have earthing added. Contact a certified electrician if necessary.",
  "faqStopcontactQ7": "Can I install an outlet in the bathroom?",
  "faqStopcontactA7": "Yes, but only outside the splash zones and with GFCI/RCD protection. Check your local wet-room regulations.",
  "faqStopcontactQ8": "How do I test if the outlet works properly?",
  "faqStopcontactA8": "After installation, switch the power back on and test the outlet with a socket tester or multimeter to check correct voltage and earthing.",
  "faqStopcontactQ9": "What is the maximum load per circuit?",
  "faqStopcontactA9": "A standard 16A circuit can handle approximately 3680W. Do not exceed the circuit capacity and spread heavy appliances across multiple circuits.",
  "faqStopcontactQ10": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqStopcontactA10": "Replacing an existing outlet is doable with good preparation. For new installations or complex wiring, consult a certified electrician.",
  "faqVerlichtingQ1": "Should I turn off the power first?",
  "faqVerlichtingA1": "Yes, always switch off the relevant circuit at the fuse box and check with a voltage tester that there is no voltage before you begin.",
  "faqVerlichtingQ2": "What is the difference between a ceiling rose and a junction box?",
  "faqVerlichtingA2": "A ceiling rose is visible and decorative; a junction box is concealed in the ceiling for the electrical connections.",
  "faqVerlichtingQ3": "How do I connect a ceiling light?",
  "faqVerlichtingA3": "Connect brown (live) to brown, blue (neutral) to blue and yellow-green (earth) to the earth terminal. Follow the light fitting instructions and local regulations.",
  "faqVerlichtingQ4": "Can I install LED spotlights myself?",
  "faqVerlichtingA4": "Yes, if you have basic electrical knowledge and follow the installation instructions. Ensure the transformer (if needed) matches the LED power rating.",
  "faqVerlichtingQ5": "What should I do if the fixture is too heavy?",
  "faqVerlichtingA5": "Use appropriate fixings (e.g. ceiling anchors or toggle bolts) or attach the light to a ceiling joist for safe installation.",
  "faqVerlichtingQ6": "Do I need a dimmer switch for LED lights?",
  "faqVerlichtingA6": "Only if the LEDs are dimmable. Use a compatible LED dimmer; standard dimmers may cause flickering.",
  "faqVerlichtingQ7": "How do I connect multiple spots in series?",
  "faqVerlichtingA7": "Connect the spots in parallel using a junction box, so each spot receives the full voltage. Do not daisy-chain in series.",
  "faqVerlichtingQ8": "What cable thickness should I use?",
  "faqVerlichtingA8": "For lighting circuits, 1.5 mm² cable is usually sufficient. For high-wattage or long runs, use 2.5 mm² as per NEN 1010.",
  "faqVerlichtingQ9": "Can I install outdoor lighting myself?",
  "faqVerlichtingA9": "Yes, provided you use weatherproof (IP-rated) fixtures and cables, and ensure GFCI/RCD protection on the circuit.",
  "faqVerlichtingQ10": "How do I test if the light works correctly?",
  "faqVerlichtingA10": "Switch the power back on, test the light switch, and check for proper operation and no flickering.",
  "faqStroomleidingQ1": "Which cable should I use for power outlets?",
  "faqStroomleidingA1": "For standard 16A power circuits use 2.5 mm² cable (3-core: live, neutral, earth). For heavier loads (cooker, etc.) use 4–6 mm².",
  "faqStroomleidingQ2": "Should I lay cables in conduit?",
  "faqStroomleidingA2": "Cables buried in walls or floors should be protected by conduit or trunking. Surface-mounted cables must also be protected.",
  "faqStroomleidingQ3": "How deep should I bury cables in the wall?",
  "faqStroomleidingA3": "Cables should be routed vertically or horizontally from outlets/switches and buried at least 50 mm deep to avoid accidental drilling.",
  "faqStroomleidingQ4": "Can I extend an existing circuit?",
  "faqStroomleidingA4": "Yes, provided the circuit is not overloaded and you follow the NEN 1010 standard. Use junction boxes for connections.",
  "faqStroomleidingQ5": "What is the difference between single-core and multi-core cable?",
  "faqStroomleidingA5": "Single-core cables are separate conductors (used in conduit); multi-core cables (e.g. 3-core) have all conductors in one sheath.",
  "faqStroomleidingQ6": "How do I connect cables in a junction box?",
  "faqStroomleidingA6": "Use connector blocks or WAGO connectors to join like colors: brown to brown, blue to blue, yellow-green to yellow-green.",
  "faqStroomleidingQ7": "Should I test the circuit after installation?",
  "faqStroomleidingA7": "Yes, always test for continuity, insulation resistance and correct earthing before energizing the circuit.",
  "faqStroomleidingQ8": "Can I run cables through the attic?",
  "faqStroomleidingA8": "Yes, but ensure cables are secured and protected from mechanical damage. Use conduit or trunking where required.",
  "faqStroomleidingQ9": "What should I do if I hit an existing cable?",
  "faqStroomleidingA9": "Switch off power immediately. Assess the damage. If the cable is nicked or cut, repair or replace the section. Never leave damaged cables live.",
  "faqStroomleidingQ10": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqStroomleidingA10": "Basic cable runs are manageable with proper planning and safety precautions. For complex installations or fuse box work, consult a certified electrician.",
  "faqKabelgootQ1": "What is cable trunking used for?",
  "faqKabelgootA1": "Cable trunking protects and conceals surface-mounted cables, keeping installations neat and safe from mechanical damage.",
  "faqKabelgootQ2": "Which size trunking do I need?",
  "faqKabelgootA2": "Choose a size that allows cables to fill no more than 60% of the trunking cross-section for heat dissipation and easy pulling.",
  "faqKabelgootQ3": "Can I install trunking on any surface?",
  "faqKabelgootA3": "Yes, trunking can be mounted on walls, skirting boards or ceilings using screws or adhesive, depending on the surface and trunking type.",
  "faqKabelgootQ4": "How do I cut trunking to size?",
  "faqKabelgootA4": "Use a fine-toothed saw or a sharp knife. Measure carefully, mark the cut line, and cut squarely for neat joints.",
  "faqKabelgootQ5": "Should I use trunking for outdoor cables?",
  "faqKabelgootA5": "Use weatherproof trunking (IP-rated) for outdoor installations to protect cables from moisture and UV damage.",
  "faqKabelgootQ6": "How do I join trunking sections?",
  "faqKabelgootA6": "Use coupling pieces or connectors supplied by the manufacturer for a secure, neat join between sections.",
  "faqKabelgootQ7": "Can I paint cable trunking?",
  "faqKabelgootA7": "Yes, most PVC trunking can be painted with suitable primer and paint to match your décor.",
  "faqKabelgootQ8": "How do I route trunking around corners?",
  "faqKabelgootA8": "Use corner pieces, bends or cut and miter the trunking at 45° for internal/external corners.",
  "faqKabelgootQ9": "Should trunking be fixed horizontally or vertically?",
  "faqKabelgootA9": "Follow the same rules as buried cables: run vertically or horizontally from outlets and switches for safety and ease of future work.",
  "faqKabelgootQ10": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqKabelgootA10": "Yes, installing trunking is straightforward with basic DIY skills. Measure carefully, cut neatly, and secure firmly.",
  "faqRookmelderQ1": "Where should I install a smoke detector?",
  "faqRookmelderA1": "Install smoke detectors on the ceiling or high on the wall (within 30 cm of the ceiling) in hallways, landings and bedrooms. Avoid kitchens and bathrooms.",
  "faqRookmelderQ2": "How many smoke detectors do I need?",
  "faqRookmelderA2": "At least one per floor and one in each bedroom. For larger homes, add more for complete coverage.",
  "faqRookmelderQ3": "Should I use battery or mains-powered detectors?",
  "faqRookmelderA3": "Mains-powered (230V) detectors with battery backup are most reliable. Battery-only detectors are easier to install but require regular battery changes.",
  "faqRookmelderQ4": "How do I install a mains-powered smoke detector?",
  "faqRookmelderA4": "Switch off the circuit, connect the detector to the lighting circuit (live, neutral, earth), secure the base to the ceiling and attach the detector head.",
  "faqRookmelderQ5": "Can I interlink multiple smoke detectors?",
  "faqRookmelderA5": "Yes, interlinked detectors (wired or wireless) sound all alarms when one detects smoke, providing better warning throughout the home.",
  "faqRookmelderQ6": "How often should I test the smoke detector?",
  "faqRookmelderA6": "Test monthly by pressing the test button. Replace batteries yearly (or when the low-battery warning sounds).",
  "faqRookmelderQ7": "What is the difference between smoke and heat detectors?",
  "faqRookmelderA7": "Smoke detectors sense smoke particles; heat detectors sense temperature rise. Use heat detectors in kitchens/garages where smoke detectors may false alarm.",
  "faqRookmelderQ8": "How long do smoke detectors last?",
  "faqRookmelderA8": "Most smoke detectors last 10 years. Check the manufacture date and replace expired units.",
  "faqRookmelderQ9": "Can I paint or decorate a smoke detector?",
  "faqRookmelderA9": "No, never paint or cover a smoke detector. This blocks the sensor and prevents it working correctly.",
  "faqRookmelderQ10": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqRookmelderA10": "Battery-powered detectors are easy to install. Mains-powered detectors require electrical knowledge; if unsure, consult a certified electrician.",
  "faqVloertegelQ1": "Do I need to level, prime and degrease?",
  "faqVloertegelA1": "Yes, check the flatness and load-bearing capacity of the substrate and level where needed. Use primer if the adhesive system or substrate requires it. Degrease greasy or contaminated substrates (e.g. kitchen floor) with a suitable degreaser/cleaner.",
  "faqVloertegelQ2": "How do I calculate cutting loss and number of tiles?",
  "faqVloertegelA2": "Calculate the surface area and divide by the area of one tile. As a guideline, allow approximately 10% cutting loss; for patterned or diagonal laying approximately 15% or more.",
  "faqVloertegelQ3": "How do I get good adhesion with adhesive?",
  "faqVloertegelA3": "Use the correct notched trowel and adhesive for the tile and substrate. Apply adhesive to a clean, dust-free substrate. For large tiles, apply back-buttering as per adhesive guidelines.",
  "faqVloertegelQ4": "How long to let dry before grouting?",
  "faqVloertegelA4": "Often a minimum of 24 hours; with large tiles, low temperatures or less absorbent substrates this can be longer. Always follow the product data sheet.",
  "faqVloertegelQ5": "How do I prevent height differences?",
  "faqVloertegelA5": "Use tile wedges or a levelling system and check regularly with a spirit level and straightedge.",
  "faqVloertegelQ6": "What to do with a loose or broken tile?",
  "faqVloertegelA6": "Carefully remove the tile, clear the substrate of loose adhesive and dust, and place a suitable new tile with appropriate adhesive.",
  "faqVloertegelQ7": "How to clean after grouting?",
  "faqVloertegelA7": "Remove grout residue at the right time with a suitable sponge and clean water, before the grout fully hardens.",
  "faqVloertegelQ8": "How do I maintain a tile floor?",
  "faqVloertegelA8": "Daily: pH-neutral tile cleaner or all-purpose cleaner. Periodically: lime/cement cleaner for stubborn deposits, but only if the tile type tolerates it. Avoid vinegar and acidic agents on natural stone, and abrasives on all tiles. Optional: impregnator on cement-based grout.",
  "faqVloertegelQ9": "What safety measures when sawing?",
  "faqVloertegelA9": "Where possible use a wet tile saw or source extraction (construction vacuum) to limit silica dust. Wear appropriate eye, ear and respiratory protection.",
  "faqVloertegelQ10": "Do I need an expansion joint for large areas?",
  "faqVloertegelA10": "For large areas or with underfloor heating, an expansion joint may be needed to accommodate movement. Follow the manufacturer's recommendations and applicable standards for maximum area without expansion.",
  "faqWandtegelQ1": "Do I need to level, prime and degrease?",
  "faqWandtegelA1": "Check that the wall is flat, clean, dry, load-bearing and grease-free. Level where needed and use primer if the substrate or adhesive system requires it. Degrease greasy or contaminated substrates (e.g. kitchen wall) with a suitable degreaser/cleaner.",
  "faqWandtegelQ2": "How do I calculate the number of tiles and cutting loss?",
  "faqWandtegelA2": "Calculate the wall surface area and divide by the area of one tile. As a guideline, allow approximately 10% extra; for patterned or diagonal laying approximately 15% or more.",
  "faqWandtegelQ3": "How do I prevent loosening?",
  "faqWandtegelA3": "Use the correct notched trowel and adhesive for the tile and substrate, don't work too much adhesive at once, and press the tiles firmly. For large tiles, back-buttering may be necessary.",
  "faqWandtegelQ4": "How long to let dry before grouting?",
  "faqWandtegelA4": "Often a minimum of 24 hours; with large tiles or unfavourable conditions this can be longer. Follow the product data sheet.",
  "faqWandtegelQ5": "How do I saw tiles without breaking?",
  "faqWandtegelA5": "Use a tile cutter for straight cuts and an angle grinder with diamond blade for cutouts. Work calmly and support the tile well.",
  "faqWandtegelQ6": "What if the wall is not level?",
  "faqWandtegelA6": "Repair or level the substrate where needed before tiling. Do not correct indefinitely with extra adhesive.",
  "faqWandtegelQ7": "How do I deal with sloped walls?",
  "faqWandtegelA7": "Measure the angle with a bevel square and cut tiles to size. Work from a square starting line and check regularly with a spirit level.",
  "faqWandtegelQ8": "How to clean after grouting?",
  "faqWandtegelA8": "Use a soft sponge and clean water at the right time before full hardening. Refresh the water regularly.",
  "faqWandtegelQ9": "How do I maintain wall tiles?",
  "faqWandtegelA9": "Daily: pH-neutral tile cleaner or mild all-purpose cleaner. For limescale: lime remover, only if the tile type tolerates it. Avoid abrasives and aggressive acids on vulnerable tiles or grout.",
  "faqWandtegelQ10": "How do I work around outlets and switches?",
  "faqWandtegelA10": "First switch off the power. Remove the cover frame, tile around the flush box to size and place the tile with a suitable cutout. Then remount the cover frame.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ1": "How do I remove an old tile without damaging the rest?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA1": "If necessary, first cut the grout around it loose. Carefully drill several holes in the centre and break out the tile piece by piece with a chisel.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ2": "Which adhesive do I use for one tile?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA2": "Use a tile adhesive or repair mortar suitable for the tile, substrate and application. Fast-drying products only as per product data sheet.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ3": "How do I get the new tile level?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA3": "Remove old adhesive residues without damaging the substrate, check flatness and use the correct notched trowel and amount of adhesive. Check with straightedge and spirit level.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ4": "How long to let dry before grouting?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA4": "This varies per adhesive. Only grout after the drying and curing time stated on the product data sheet; for fast-drying adhesive only follow that explicit product instruction.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ5": "How do I finish the grout seamlessly?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA5": "Use the same or demonstrably matching grout and colour. Fill the joint completely and finish according to the manufacturer.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ6": "What if the substrate is damaged?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA6": "First repair the substrate with a suitable repair or levelling compound. Allow this to cure sufficiently.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ7": "How do I prevent adhesive residue?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA7": "Remove excess adhesive immediately in the manner prescribed by the manufacturer, without damaging the tile or grout.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ8": "How do I maintain the repaired tile?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA8": "Maintain the tile in the same way as the rest of the floor or wall: pH-neutral tile cleaner or a mild detergent. Avoid abrasives and aggressive acids.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ9": "What safety measures?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA9": "Limit dust when drilling and breaking with source extraction (construction vacuum) or wet processing. Wear appropriate eye and respiratory protection.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ10": "Can I repair a tile without damaging surrounding tiles?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA10": "Yes, with careful working. Cut the grout around it loose, remove the tile piece by piece and protect adjacent tiles with a board or cloth.",
  "faqVoegenQ1": "Do joints need to be clean and dry?",
  "faqVoegenA1": "Yes. Remove adhesive residues and dirt from the joints. The joints must meet the dryness and processing conditions of the chosen grout.",
  "faqVoegenQ2": "Which grout do I use indoors?",
  "faqVoegenA2": "Choose based on tile type, joint width and application. Indoors, cement-based or epoxy grout may be suitable. Follow the product data sheet.",
  "faqVoegenQ3": "How do I mix grout?",
  "faqVoegenA3": "Follow exactly the mixing ratio and working time on the packaging. Use clean water and a suitable mixer.",
  "faqVoegenQ4": "How long to cure before loading?",
  "faqVoegenA4": "This varies per product, temperature and application. Observe the stated waiting time for loading and full curing on the product data sheet.",
  "faqVoegenQ5": "How do I prevent drying out or cracking?",
  "faqVoegenA5": "Work in small sections and avoid drafts, direct sun and too rapid drying. Only moisten if the manufacturer prescribes it.",
  "faqVoegenQ6": "What to do with unevenness in the joint?",
  "faqVoegenA6": "Finish the joint within the permitted working time with the correct tools. After curing, rework is often visible; follow product instructions for repair.",
  "faqVoegenQ7": "How do I clean tiles without damaging joints?",
  "faqVoegenA7": "Clean at the right time with a suitable sponge and limited clean water. Don't wait until a hard cement haze has formed.",
  "faqVoegenQ8": "How do I maintain grout?",
  "faqVoegenA8": "Cement-based grout: grout cleaner or green soap. Epoxy grout: pH-neutral only. Optional: impregnator on cement-based grout. Avoid aggressive acids unless the product is explicitly suitable for the grout type.",
  "faqVoegenQ9": "What safety measures?",
  "faqVoegenA9": "Wear suitable gloves and eye protection. Limit dust when mixing powder products and ensure ventilation.",
  "faqVoegenQ10": "Can I rework joints after curing?",
  "faqVoegenA10": "After full curing, rework is usually no longer possible without visible results. Therefore finish the joint within the working time of the product.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ1": "Do I need to degrease and dry?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA1": "Yes. Make the substrate clean, grease-free and dust-free and let it dry, unless the product explicitly prescribes otherwise.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ2": "Which sealant do I use?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA2": "Silicone sealant is suitable for many sanitary and wet joints. Acrylic sealant is often used for paintable joints indoors.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ3": "How do I remove old sealant?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA3": "Cut or scrape old sealant carefully away with a sealant scraper and use a suitable sealant remover if needed. Then clean according to the instructions of the new sealant product.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ4": "How do I apply sealant evenly?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA4": "Cut the nozzle diagonally to the desired joint width and apply the sealant evenly. Smooth with a sealant smoother (rubber) or suitable sealant spatula. Use a spray bottle with a smoothing agent approved by the manufacturer.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ5": "How long to let dry before loading?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA5": "This varies per sealant type, joint size and conditions. Follow the skin formation, curing and loadability times on the packaging.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ6": "How do I smooth sealant?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA6": "Use a sealant smoother (rubber) or suitable sealant spatula and a smoothing agent approved by the manufacturer. Do not automatically use washing-up liquid.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ7": "What if sealant does not adhere?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA7": "Remove the sealant, check that the substrate is suitable and clean and dry it again. Use the prescribed primer if necessary.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ8": "How do I maintain sealant edges?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA8": "Check regularly for loosening, cracks, mould or discolouration. For mould: use a suitable sealant or mould cleaner that matches the sealant type, or replace the sealant edge. Avoid aggressive agents that attack the sealant.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ9": "What safety measures?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA9": "Ensure adequate ventilation, wear suitable gloves and avoid skin and eye contact.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ10": "Can I apply sealant over old sealant?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA10": "No, this is not recommended. Old sealant may come loose or contain mould. Remove the old sealant completely, clean the substrate and apply new sealant according to the manufacturer.",
  "faqPlumberQ1": "How do I fix a dripping faucet?",
  "faqPlumberA1": "Usually by replacing the washer or cartridge inside the faucet.",
  "faqPlumberQ2": "How do I unblock a drain?",
  "faqPlumberA2": "Use a plunger, drain snake or suitable drain cleaner.",
  "faqPlumberQ3": "What is PEX piping?",
  "faqPlumberA3": "PEX is flexible plastic piping commonly used for water supply lines.",
  "faqPlumberQ4": "How do I detect leaks?",
  "faqPlumberA4": "Look for water stains, damp spots or unexpected increases in water bills.",
  "faqPlumberQ5": "Should I use Teflon tape?",
  "faqPlumberA5": "Yes, on threaded connections to improve sealing.",
  "faqPlumberQ6": "Can I replace a toilet myself?",
  "faqPlumberA6": "Yes, many homeowners can replace a toilet with basic tools.",
  "faqPlumberQ7": "How much water pressure is normal?",
  "faqPlumberA7": "Most homes operate between 2 and 5 bar.",
  "faqPlumberQ8": "How do I prevent frozen pipes?",
  "faqPlumberA8": "Insulate exposed pipes and maintain indoor temperatures.",
  "faqPlumberQ9": "What are common plumbing mistakes?",
  "faqPlumberA9": "Overtightening fittings and forgetting to check for leaks.",
  "faqPlumberQ10": "How often should pipes be inspected?",
  "faqPlumberA10": "A yearly inspection helps detect issues early.",
  "faqPlumberQ11": "What tools are essential for beginners?",
  "faqPlumberA11": "Adjustable wrench, pliers, pipe cutter and Teflon tape.",
  "faqPlumberQ12": "Can I install a new sink myself?",
  "faqPlumberA12": "Yes, if plumbing connections are straightforward.",
  "faqPlumberQ13": "How do I reduce water consumption?",
  "faqPlumberA13": "Install water-saving fixtures and repair leaks promptly.",
  "faqPlumberQ14": "How long do plumbing repairs last?",
  "faqPlumberA14": "Quality repairs can last many years when done correctly.",
  "faqPlumberQ15": "When should I call a professional?",
  "faqPlumberA15": "For gas lines, major leaks or extensive pipe replacements.",
  "faqPlumberQ16": "How long does this job take?",
  "faqPlumberA16": "Depending on the complexity, a plumbing job takes 1–8 hours. Replacing a simple faucet takes about 30 minutes; laying a pipe can take a day or more.",
  "faqPlumberQ17": "What tools do I need?",
  "faqPlumberA17": "Basic tools: water pump pliers, wrenches, pipe cutter, Teflon tape, soldering torch (for copper work) and a bucket.",
  "faqPlumberQ18": "Do I need to shut off the water supply?",
  "faqPlumberA18": "Yes, always shut off the water supply before starting. Use the main valve or a local shut-off valve and then open a tap to release the pressure.",
  "faqPlumberQ19": "What type of pipe material should I choose?",
  "faqPlumberA19": "Copper is durable but more expensive. PEX is flexible and easy to work with. PVC is widely used for drain pipes. Choose based on the application.",
  "faqPlumberQ20": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqPlumberA20": "Simple jobs like replacing a faucet or repairing a toilet are perfectly doable on your own. More complex work (gas, new pipework) is better left to a certified plumber.",
  "faqElectricianQ1": "What is a circuit breaker?",
  "faqElectricianA1": "A safety device that cuts power when overloads occur.",
  "faqElectricianQ2": "How do I test a power outlet?",
  "faqElectricianA2": "Use a socket tester or multimeter.",
  "faqElectricianQ3": "Can I replace a light fixture myself?",
  "faqElectricianA3": "Yes, after switching off power and following safety procedures.",
  "faqElectricianQ4": "Why does a breaker trip?",
  "faqElectricianA4": "Usually because of overloads, short circuits or faulty appliances.",
  "faqElectricianQ5": "What is grounding?",
  "faqElectricianA5": "Grounding provides a safe path for electrical faults.",
  "faqElectricianQ6": "How do I identify wires?",
  "faqElectricianA6": "Use local wiring color codes and verify with a tester.",
  "faqElectricianQ7": "Can I add extra outlets?",
  "faqElectricianA7": "Yes, provided the circuit can handle the load.",
  "faqElectricianQ8": "What PPE should I wear?",
  "faqElectricianA8": "Insulated gloves and safety glasses are recommended.",
  "faqElectricianQ9": "How do I avoid electric shock?",
  "faqElectricianA9": "Always isolate power before starting work.",
  "faqElectricianQ10": "How long do electrical installations last?",
  "faqElectricianA10": "Many installations last 20–40 years with proper maintenance.",
  "faqElectricianQ11": "What is a GFCI/RCD?",
  "faqElectricianA11": "A device that protects against electric shock.",
  "faqElectricianQ12": "Can I install outdoor lighting?",
  "faqElectricianA12": "Yes, using weatherproof fixtures and cables.",
  "faqElectricianQ13": "What are common electrical mistakes?",
  "faqElectricianA13": "Working live and using incorrect wire sizes.",
  "faqElectricianQ14": "How do I calculate power consumption?",
  "faqElectricianA14": "Multiply watts by hours of use and divide by 1000.",
  "faqElectricianQ15": "When should I call an electrician?",
  "faqElectricianA15": "For extensions to the fuse box, new wiring or complex installations, it is wise to call a certified electrician.",
  "faqElectricianQ16": "How long does this job take?",
  "faqElectricianA16": "Installing an outlet or switch takes 30–60 minutes. Larger projects such as adding a new circuit can take half to a full day.",
  "faqElectricianQ17": "What tools do I need?",
  "faqElectricianA17": "You need: a screwdriver set, wire puller, side cutters, multimeter, insulation tape and possibly a hammer drill.",
  "faqElectricianQ18": "How do I work safely?",
  "faqElectricianA18": "Always turn off the power via the fuse box and check with a voltage tester that the circuit is truly de-energized before starting.",
  "faqElectricianQ19": "What wire thickness should I use?",
  "faqElectricianA19": "For outlets and lighting: 2.5 mm² for normal circuits. For heavier appliances (hob, washing machine): 4–6 mm². Consult the NEN 1010 standard.",
  "faqElectricianQ20": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqElectricianA20": "Simple replacements (switch, outlet) can be done independently if you follow safety rules. Work on the fuse box or new circuits should be left to a certified electrician.",
  "faqTilerQ1": "Which tile adhesive should I use?",
  "faqTilerA1": "Choose adhesive based on the tile type and substrate. Flexible adhesive is recommended for most modern installations.",
  "faqTilerQ2": "Do I need to waterproof the surface?",
  "faqTilerA2": "Yes, bathrooms and wet areas should be waterproofed before tiling.",
  "faqTilerQ3": "How do I keep tiles level?",
  "faqTilerA3": "Use tile spacers and a leveling system to ensure an even surface.",
  "faqTilerQ4": "When can I walk on newly laid tiles?",
  "faqTilerA4": "Wait at least 24 hours after installation before walking on the tiles.",
  "faqTilerQ5": "How long before grouting?",
  "faqTilerA5": "Most tile adhesives require 24 hours of curing before grouting.",
  "faqTilerQ6": "What grout color should I choose?",
  "faqTilerA6": "Choose a grout color that complements your tile or creates the desired contrast.",
  "faqTilerQ7": "How do I cut tiles accurately?",
  "faqTilerA7": "Use a tile cutter for straight cuts and a wet saw for complex shapes.",
  "faqTilerQ8": "Can I tile over existing tiles?",
  "faqTilerA8": "Yes, if the existing tiles are stable, clean and properly prepared.",
  "faqTilerQ9": "What are common tiling mistakes?",
  "faqTilerA9": "Uneven adhesive application, poor spacing and insufficient surface preparation.",
  "faqTilerQ10": "How do I clean grout haze?",
  "faqTilerA10": "Use a damp sponge shortly after grouting and a grout haze remover if necessary.",
  "faqTilerQ11": "How much grout do I need?",
  "faqTilerA11": "The amount depends on tile size, thickness and joint width.",
  "faqTilerQ12": "Should I seal grout?",
  "faqTilerA12": "Sealing grout helps prevent stains and moisture penetration.",
  "faqTilerQ13": "Can beginners tile a bathroom?",
  "faqTilerA13": "Yes, but start with simple layouts and take time with preparation.",
  "faqTilerQ14": "How do I replace a broken tile?",
  "faqTilerA14": "Carefully remove the damaged tile and install a matching replacement.",
  "faqTilerQ15": "How long do tiled surfaces last?",
  "faqTilerA15": "Properly installed tiles can last several decades with minimal maintenance.",
  "faqTilerQ16": "How long does tiling take?",
  "faqTilerA16": "Tiling a bathroom takes an average of 1–3 days. Tiling a small floor or wall takes about 4–8 hours, depending on the complexity.",
  "faqTilerQ17": "What tools do I need?",
  "faqTilerA17": "You need: tile cutter, notched trowel, grout float, rubber mallet, spirit level, tape measure, sponge and tile spacers.",
  "faqTilerQ18": "How do I prepare the substrate?",
  "faqTilerA18": "The substrate must be dry, flat and clean. Check for height differences with a spirit level. Apply tile adhesive to an even substrate.",
  "faqTilerQ19": "How many extra tiles do I need?",
  "faqTilerA19": "Always order 10–15% extra. For diagonal or herringbone patterns, allow even 20% extra for cutting loss.",
  "faqTilerQ20": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqTilerA20": "Simple tiling can be done independently with the right preparation. Start with a straight wall or floor without many niches or obstacles.",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenQ1": "Do I need a permit to install a fence?",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenA1": "Check in advance via the Omgevingsloket and/or your local council whether a permit or notification is required for your location, height, boundary, and construction.",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenQ2": "How many posts and what is the correct spacing?",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenA2": "The number of posts and their spacing depend on the chosen system, panel width and wind load. Follow the manufacturer's specifications.",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenQ3": "How deep do the posts need to go?",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenA3": "As a general guideline, approximately 1/3 of the post length should be underground, but the required foundation depth depends on soil, post thickness, wind load and the chosen system. Always follow the specifications of the chosen system.",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenQ4": "Which post thickness should I choose?",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenA4": "Choose a thicker post for a robust appearance or in high wind areas. Follow the advice of the manufacturer or supplier.",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenQ5": "How long must the concrete cure before loading the fence?",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenA5": "Follow the specifications of the concrete product used. Curing time depends on temperature, moisture and product; 24 hours is not automatically sufficient for full load.",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenQ6": "How do I ensure the fence is level?",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenA6": "Use a spirit level and guide line, each post at equal height. Check regularly. Use a suitable and safe ladder or platform for placing the top panels.",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenQ7": "What if the ground is too soft?",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenA7": "Extra concrete or dig deeper. Consider a post holder. Follow the system advice.",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenQ8": "How do I maintain the fence?",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenA8": "Wood: check periodically and treat according to the maintenance advice for the wood type or manufacturer (e.g. wood stain or wood oil). Plastic: rinse with water.",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenQ9": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenA9": "Not deep enough, not level, too few posts or insufficient foundation, system specifications not followed.",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenQ10": "Best tip for a sturdy fence?",
  "faqSchuttingPlaatsenA10": "Posts in concrete according to the system, use stainless steel screws. Always follow the manufacturer's specifications.",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenQ1": "Do I need to prepare the ground?",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenA1": "Yes. Excavate to the required depth, remove old paving and level the surface. A weed control membrane is not always necessary; only use it if appropriate for your application.",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenQ2": "How much material do I need?",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenA2": "Calculate the area in m² and determine the quantity of gravel tiles based on 60 x 60 cm per tile. Allow for project-specific cutting waste; with a lot of sawing, more than 5% is needed.",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenQ3": "How do I make a good sand bed?",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenA3": "The required foundation and sand layer depend on the load, subsoil and type of paving. Apply the correct layer thickness, compact it well and ensure a stable base.",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenQ4": "How do I lay the paving?",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenA4": "Lay the blocks tightly against each other and tap them down with a rubber mallet. Check for height differences regularly.",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenQ5": "How long should the sand bed rest?",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenA5": "You can start laying immediately after compacting, provided the sand bed is well compacted. Work in small sections.",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenQ6": "How do I fix a sunken area?",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenA6": "Lift the sunken tiles, top up the sand bed, compact it again and replace the tiles level.",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenQ7": "How do I maintain the paving?",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenA7": "Sweep regularly, remove weeds between the joints and top up the jointing sand if necessary.",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenQ8": "What safety precautions apply?",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenA8": "Gloves and safety glasses. Wear a dust mask when dust is released, for example during dry sawing.",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenQ9": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenA9": "Insufficient foundation or compaction, incorrect drainage, wrong layer structure, insufficient slope.",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenQ10": "Best tip for a sleek result?",
  "faqBestratingAanleggenA10": "Maintain sufficient slope for good water drainage; the required gradient depends on the application and subsoil. Check with a spirit level.",
  "faqSnoeienQ1": "When is the best time to prune?",
  "faqSnoeienA1": "The correct pruning time and method differ per hydrangea species. First check which species you have. Then follow the advice for that specific species.",
  "faqSnoeienQ2": "What tools do I need?",
  "faqSnoeienA2": "Pruning shears for thin branches, loppers or pruning saw for thicker branches. Sharp tools.",
  "faqSnoeienQ3": "How many branches should I remove?",
  "faqSnoeienA3": "Do not prune too much at once. Remove dead, diseased or damaged branches and old flowers. The approach differs per species (mophead hydrangea versus panicle hydrangea).",
  "faqSnoeienQ4": "How exactly should I prune?",
  "faqSnoeienA4": "Cut just above a new green bud or growing point, at an angle away from the bud. Work from the inside out.",
  "faqSnoeienQ5": "How do I repair damage from pruning?",
  "faqSnoeienA5": "Prune damaged branches back to healthy wood. Let the plant rest.",
  "faqSnoeienQ6": "How do I maintain plants after pruning?",
  "faqSnoeienA6": "Water the plant after pruning only when necessary and apply fertiliser according to the hydrangea's needs. Remove pruning waste.",
  "faqSnoeienQ7": "What safety precautions apply?",
  "faqSnoeienA7": "Gloves, safety glasses, stable ladder for high work. Watch out for falling branches.",
  "faqSnoeienQ8": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqSnoeienA8": "Wrong period, wrong pruning method for the species, blunt tools, not clearing waste.",
  "faqSnoeienQ9": "Best tip for pruning?",
  "faqSnoeienA9": "Preferably do not prune during frost or when severe frost is expected directly after pruning. First check which hydrangea species you have and follow the advice for that species.",
  "faqSnoeienQ10": "Should I treat pruning wounds with a product?",
  "faqSnoeienA10": "No, the use of wound sealants is not recommended as standard for hydrangeas. The plant heals best naturally after a clean, sharp cut.",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenQ1": "Do I need a permit?",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenA1": "Check beforehand with your local authority whether a permit or notification is required for the canopy. Also check any local association rules.",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenQ2": "How many posts do I need and how heavy should they be?",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenA2": "The number and dimensions of posts, beams and connections depend on the dimensions, span, roof load, wind load and foundation. Consult a structural engineer if in doubt.",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenQ3": "How do I build a sturdy frame?",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenA3": "Use the correct wood dimensions and connections according to the structural calculation. Ensure a level and stable frame. Consult a structural engineer if in doubt.",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenQ4": "How long should the concrete dry?",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenA4": "Follow the specifications of the concrete product used. Curing time depends on temperature, moisture and product; 24 hours is not automatically sufficient for full load.",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenQ5": "How do I prevent the wood from rotting or discolouring?",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenA5": "Choose a suitable wood type or another material (plastic/aluminium). Treat according to the manufacturer's maintenance advice.",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenQ6": "How do I ensure good drainage?",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenA6": "Maintain sufficient slope for good water drainage; the required gradient depends on application and roofing material. Follow the specifications of the roofing material.",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenQ7": "How do I maintain the canopy?",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenA7": "Check annually for damage and clean the roof. Treat woodwork according to the maintenance advice for the wood type or manufacturer.",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenQ8": "What safety precautions apply?",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenA8": "Helmet, gloves, scaffolding. Never work at height alone. Use a suitable and safe ladder or platform for lower work.",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenQ9": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenA9": "Insufficient or incorrectly executed structure, not level, wrong roof pitch, no good foundation, forgetting the permit.",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenQ10": "Best tip for a good canopy?",
  "faqOverkappingBouwenA10": "Maintain sufficient slope for rain drainage. Check everything level. Consult a structural engineer if in doubt.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ1": "Why choose artificial grass?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA1": "Artificial grass generally requires less maintenance than natural grass and stays green all year round. Regular cleaning and brushing is still needed. Choose a quality that suits the usage.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ2": "Do I need to prepare the ground?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA2": "Yes, level and compact. Remove old turf, weeds and unevenness. Apply a stable base layer.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ3": "How much artificial grass do I need?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA3": "Area + cutting waste (minimum 5%, more with extensive cutting). Artificial grass is usually supplied on rolls with a fixed width.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ4": "How do I lay artificial grass?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA4": "Roll out the artificial grass, let it acclimatise to the temperature, cut to size and lay the seams tightly together. Optionally use seam tape.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ5": "How do I maintain artificial grass?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA5": "Brush regularly, remove leaves and rinse with water. Do not use aggressive products.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ6": "What should I do about puddles or poor drainage?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA6": "Ensure a permeable base layer. A drainage system may be needed in heavy rain.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ7": "What safety precautions apply?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA7": "Gloves when cutting. Watch out for heavy equipment. Work stably.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ8": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA8": "Insufficient levelling, no stable base layer, seams not tight, incorrect cutting technique.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ9": "Best tip for laying artificial grass?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA9": "Let the artificial grass lie for a few hours before laying so it can settle. Cut with a sharp knife and a straight edge.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ10": "Can I place a barbecue or fire pit on artificial grass?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA10": "No, artificial grass is made of plastic and can melt from splashing sparks or direct heat. Always keep open flames and hot objects at a safe distance.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ9": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA9": "Not levelling the ground properly, watering too little or irregularly, laying at the wrong time of year and not pressing the ground down enough.",
  "faqGazonLeggenQ10": "Best tip for a beautiful lawn?",
  "faqGazonLeggenA10": "Lay or sow the lawn in spring or early autumn for the best growth and rooting. Avoid laying during hot, dry summer months.",
  "faqGardenerQ1": "How often should I water plants?",
  "faqGardenerA1": "Most plants benefit from deep watering 1–3 times per week depending on weather conditions.",
  "faqGardenerQ2": "What type of soil is best?",
  "faqGardenerA2": "Well-draining soil enriched with compost is suitable for most garden plants.",
  "faqGardenerQ3": "How do I improve soil quality?",
  "faqGardenerA3": "Add compost, organic matter and mulch regularly.",
  "faqGardenerQ4": "When should I fertilize?",
  "faqGardenerA4": "Most plants benefit from fertilizing during the growing season.",
  "faqGardenerQ5": "How do I control weeds?",
  "faqGardenerA5": "Mulching and regular weeding are the most effective methods.",
  "faqGardenerQ6": "Can I plant during summer?",
  "faqGardenerA6": "Yes, but plants may require more frequent watering.",
  "faqGardenerQ7": "What is mulch used for?",
  "faqGardenerA7": "Mulch retains moisture, suppresses weeds and improves soil health.",
  "faqGardenerQ8": "How do I prune shrubs correctly?",
  "faqGardenerA8": "Remove dead or damaged branches and prune according to the plant type.",
  "faqGardenerQ9": "How can I attract pollinators?",
  "faqGardenerA9": "Plant flowering species that attract bees and butterflies.",
  "faqGardenerQ10": "How do I prevent pests naturally?",
  "faqGardenerA10": "Encourage beneficial insects and use organic pest control methods.",
  "faqGardenerQ11": "When is the best time to mow the lawn?",
  "faqGardenerA11": "Mow regularly during the growing season and avoid cutting too short.",
  "faqGardenerQ12": "How do I start a vegetable garden?",
  "faqGardenerA12": "Choose a sunny location, prepare the soil and select suitable crops.",
  "faqGardenerQ13": "Can I garden in containers?",
  "faqGardenerA13": "Yes, container gardening is ideal for patios and small spaces.",
  "faqGardenerQ14": "How do I reduce garden maintenance?",
  "faqGardenerA14": "Use mulch, drought-tolerant plants and automated irrigation where possible.",
  "faqGardenerQ15": "What are common gardening mistakes?",
  "faqGardenerA15": "Overwatering, poor plant spacing and neglecting soil preparation.",
  "faqGardenerQ16": "How long does this garden job take?",
  "faqGardenerA16": "This varies greatly per job. Laying a lawn takes half a day; laying a terrace can take 1–3 days, depending on the size.",
  "faqGardenerQ17": "What tools do I need?",
  "faqGardenerA17": "Basic tools: spade, rake, wheelbarrow, pruning shears and garden gloves. Specific jobs may require additional tools.",
  "faqGardenerQ18": "When is the best time for garden work?",
  "faqGardenerA18": "Spring and autumn are ideal times for planting and major work. Avoid extreme heat or frost.",
  "faqGardenerQ19": "How do I check if my soil is good?",
  "faqGardenerA19": "Take a soil sample and test the pH value. Most plants grow best at a pH of 6–7. Add compost to improve soil quality.",
  "faqGardenerQ20": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqGardenerA20": "Yes, most garden jobs can be done independently with the right tools and our step-by-step guide.",
  "faqCarpenterQ1": "What type of wood should I choose?",
  "faqCarpenterA1": "The best choice depends on the project. Pine is affordable, while hardwoods are more durable.",
  "faqCarpenterQ2": "How do I measure accurately?",
  "faqCarpenterA2": "Measure twice and cut once to avoid costly mistakes.",
  "faqCarpenterQ3": "Should I pre-drill holes?",
  "faqCarpenterA3": "Yes, pre-drilling helps prevent wood from splitting.",
  "faqCarpenterQ4": "What screws should I use?",
  "faqCarpenterA4": "Summary for the average DIYer:\nOutdoor = Stainless steel (RVS) with Torx, length is 2.5x timber thickness, and thickness is 6mm for rafters/battens and 8mm+ for heavy structural beams. Indoor = affordable galvanized screws with the same length rule.",
  "faqCarpenterQ5": "How do I keep cuts straight?",
  "faqCarpenterA5": "Use a guide rail, square or fence when cutting.",
  "faqCarpenterQ6": "Can I use reclaimed wood?",
  "faqCarpenterA6": "Yes, provided it is clean, dry and structurally sound.",
  "faqCarpenterQ7": "How do I prevent wood from warping?",
  "faqCarpenterA7": "Store wood flat in a dry environment before use.",
  "faqCarpenterQ8": "What safety equipment should I wear?",
  "faqCarpenterA8": "Safety glasses, hearing protection and dust masks are recommended.",
  "faqCarpenterQ9": "How do I sand wood properly?",
  "faqCarpenterA9": "Start with coarse grit and gradually move to finer grits.",
  "faqCarpenterQ10": "When should I use wood glue?",
  "faqCarpenterA10": "Wood glue strengthens joints and is recommended for most woodworking projects.",
  "faqCarpenterQ11": "How do I finish wood surfaces?",
  "faqCarpenterA11": "Apply stain, varnish, oil or paint depending on the desired appearance.",
  "faqCarpenterQ12": "What are common carpentry mistakes?",
  "faqCarpenterA12": "Incorrect measurements, poor alignment and rushing the assembly process.",
  "faqCarpenterQ13": "How long does a woodworking project last?",
  "faqCarpenterA13": "With proper materials and maintenance, woodworking projects can last decades.",
  "faqCarpenterQ14": "How long does this job take?",
  "faqCarpenterA14": "On average, allow half to a full day for a standard carpentry job. For larger projects this can run to several days.",
  "faqCarpenterQ15": "What tools do I need?",
  "faqCarpenterA15": "Basic tools: tape measure, spirit level, pencil, drill, screws, saw and hammer.",
  "faqCarpenterQ16": "How do I prepare the substrate?",
  "faqCarpenterA16": "Check that the floor or wall is flat and sturdy. Remove loose parts and ensure the surface is dry and clean.",
  "faqCarpenterQ17": "How much extra material should I order?",
  "faqCarpenterA17": "Always order 10% extra for cutting loss. Enable the \"cutting loss\" option in the app to calculate this automatically.",
  "faqCarpenterQ18": "Can I do this as a beginner?",
  "faqCarpenterA18": "Yes, with the right preparation and our step-by-step guide you can carry out this job independently.",
  "faqCarpenterQ19": "Do I need a permit?",
  "faqCarpenterA19": "A permit is not required for most DIY jobs indoors. For outdoor work or structural changes, consult your municipality.",
  "faqCarpenterQ20": "Which DIY store do you recommend?",
  "faqCarpenterA20": "For DIY homeowners, we recommend consumer home centers like Gamma and Hornbach (or Home Depot and Lowe's). For professional contractors and builders, trade wholesalers like Bouwmaat and Jongeneel (or Ferguson and White Cap) provide specialist construction materials and bulk supplies.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ1": "Should I acclimatise the laminate flooring?",
  "faqVloerleggenA1": "Follow the manufacturer's instructions; often 48 hours.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ2": "What if the subfloor is not level?",
  "faqVloerleggenA2": "Level it or use a suitable underlay.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ3": "Do I need underlay?",
  "faqVloerleggenA3": "Usually yes, for sound insulation and stability.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ4": "How much space should I leave at the walls?",
  "faqVloerleggenA4": "Maintain the edge clearance specified by the manufacturer.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ5": "Do I need to wait before walking on the floor?",
  "faqVloerleggenA5": "Follow the click-lock system's instructions; many systems can be walked on immediately.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ6": "How do I prevent creaking?",
  "faqVloerleggenA6": "Ensure a level subfloor and correct installation.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ7": "What should I do if a plank is damaged?",
  "faqVloerleggenA7": "Replace it according to the manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ8": "How do I maintain the floor?",
  "faqVloerleggenA8": "Use a suitable cleaning product and do not make the floor too wet.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ9": "What safety precautions are required?",
  "faqVloerleggenA9": "Wear eye and hearing protection when sawing.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqVloerleggenA10": "Read the installation instructions and check the subfloor.",
  "faqVloerleggenQ11": "How do I maintain the floor?",
  "faqVloerleggenA11": "Use a suitable cleaning product and do not make the floor too wet.",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenQ1": "Which type of interior door?",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenA1": "Flush door or rebated door.",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenQ2": "How do I measure the door and frame?",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenA2": "Measure the structural opening and clearance according to the manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenQ3": "Where should the hinges go?",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenA3": "Follow the manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenQ4": "How do I prevent the door from sticking?",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenA4": "Ensure the correct clearance and alignment.",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenQ5": "What if the door does not hang properly?",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenA5": "Check the frame, hinges, and clearance.",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenQ6": "Do I need to wait before painting?",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenA6": "Follow the paint manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenQ7": "How do I prevent warping?",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenA7": "Apply proper primer and finishing.",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenQ8": "How do I maintain the hinges and locking hardware?",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenA8": "Follow the manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenQ9": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenA9": "Incorrect measurements, clearance, or hardware.",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqDeurPlaatsenA10": "Use a door lifter and work safely.",
  "faqPlintenMonerenQ1": "Do I need to clean the wall and floor?",
  "faqPlintenMonerenA1": "Yes. The surfaces must be clean, dry, and free from dust and grease for the selected fixing method.",
  "faqPlintenMonerenQ2": "How do I make a 45-degree mitre cut?",
  "faqPlintenMonerenA2": "Use a mitre box or mitre saw and determine the actual angle with a sliding bevel when the corner is not exactly 90 degrees.",
  "faqPlintenMonerenQ3": "How many metres of skirting board do I need?",
  "faqPlintenMonerenA3": "Measure the total length to be covered and generally add about 10% for cutting waste. More may be needed when there are many corners.",
  "faqPlintenMonerenQ4": "How do I prevent the skirting board from coming loose?",
  "faqPlintenMonerenA4": "Use the correct fixing method for the type of skirting board and substrate. Clean the substrate and follow the instructions for the adhesive or tape.",
  "faqPlintenMonerenQ5": "How do I deal with uneven surfaces?",
  "faqPlintenMonerenA5": "For continuous skirting boards, small gaps can be finished with suitable acrylic sealant. Large gaps require an appropriate solution; do not use sealant as a structural filler.",
  "faqPlintenMonerenQ6": "How long does the skirting board need to dry?",
  "faqPlintenMonerenA6": "Follow the drying and curing time of the adhesive or sealant being used. Tape/adhesive skirting systems may behave differently from glued or mechanically fixed skirting boards.",
  "faqPlintenMonerenQ7": "What if the skirting board does not fit tightly?",
  "faqPlintenMonerenA7": "Check the corner, floor, and wall. Small cosmetic gaps can be appropriately finished; larger deviations should be resolved first.",
  "faqPlintenMonerenQ8": "How do I maintain skirting boards?",
  "faqPlintenMonerenA8": "Clean them with a surface-appropriate damp cloth and repair any damage according to the manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqPlintenMonerenQ9": "What safety measures are needed?",
  "faqPlintenMonerenA9": "Use eye protection when using power saws and appropriate respiratory protection when dust is produced. Keep your hands away from the cutting line.",
  "faqPlintenMonerenQ10": "What is the best tip for non-square corners?",
  "faqPlintenMonerenA10": "Measure the actual angle and first test it with a short test piece before cutting the final skirting board.",
  "faqPlakplintQ1": "Should I clean the wall and floor?",
  "faqPlakplintA1": "Yes, they must be clean, dry, and dust-free.",
  "faqPlakplintQ2": "How do I cut a mitre?",
  "faqPlakplintA2": "Use a mitre box or mitre saw and measure the angle.",
  "faqPlakplintQ3": "How many metres of skirting do I need?",
  "faqPlakplintA3": "Perimeter plus 10% for cutting waste.",
  "faqPlakplintQ4": "How do I prevent the skirting from coming loose?",
  "faqPlakplintA4": "Ensure the floor is clean and dry, and press firmly.",
  "faqPlakplintQ5": "How do I hide uneven areas?",
  "faqPlakplintA5": "Do not use sealant for this; ensure the floor is level.",
  "faqPlakplintQ6": "Do I need to wait before putting pressure on the skirting?",
  "faqPlakplintA6": "It is ready immediately; follow the adhesive or sealant instructions.",
  "faqPlakplintQ7": "What if the skirting does not fit tightly?",
  "faqPlakplintA7": "Check the floor; use filler for large gaps.",
  "faqPlakplintQ8": "How do I maintain skirting boards?",
  "faqPlakplintA8": "Wipe with a damp cloth.",
  "faqPlakplintQ9": "What safety precautions are required?",
  "faqPlakplintA9": "Wear eye and hearing protection when sawing.",
  "faqPlakplintQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqPlakplintA10": "Test the corner with a sample piece first.",
  "faqVoorzetplintQ1": "Do I need to degrease the wall before gluing?",
  "faqVoorzetplintA1": "Yes, a degreased and dust-free surface is essential for good adhesion of the glue or mounting adhesive. Use a degreaser and allow the wall to dry completely.",
  "faqVoorzetplintQ2": "How do I cut a 45-degree mitre?",
  "faqVoorzetplintA2": "Use a mitre box or electric mitre saw. Saw with the decorative face up for a clean finish. Always dry-fit the corner before fixing it permanently.",
  "faqVoorzetplintQ3": "How many metres of skirting board do I need?",
  "faqVoorzetplintA3": "Measure the perimeter of the room and add 10% for cutting waste at corners.",
  "faqVoorzetplintQ4": "How do I prevent the overlay skirting board from coming loose?",
  "faqVoorzetplintA4": "Use the correct mounting adhesive or double-sided tape and ensure the wall is dust-free, grease-free, and dry. Screws provide extra security for heavy boards.",
  "faqVoorzetplintQ5": "How do I fill gaps and unevenness?",
  "faqVoorzetplintA5": "Seal the joints around the board with acrylic caulk and smooth it with a wet finger or finishing tool for a neat result.",
  "faqVoorzetplintQ6": "How long must the caulk dry before painting?",
  "faqVoorzetplintA6": "Acrylic caulk is usually touch-dry within 1 hour and fully cured after 24 hours. Follow the drying time stated on the product packaging.",
  "faqVoorzetplintQ7": "What if the overlay board does not sit flush against the wall?",
  "faqVoorzetplintA7": "Apply extra caulk or use a filler strip. For larger gaps, expanding foam or an additional wood strip is a better solution than caulk alone.",
  "faqVoorzetplintQ8": "How do I maintain overlay skirting boards?",
  "faqVoorzetplintA8": "Wipe regularly with a lightly damp cloth. For lacquered boards, touch up any damage with matching lacquer. Check the caulk joint annually.",
  "faqVoorzetplintQ9": "What safety measures apply when sawing?",
  "faqVoorzetplintA9": "Wear a dust mask and work gloves. Always use a sharp blade for a clean, dust-free cut.",
  "faqVoorzetplintQ10": "What is the best tip for invisible corners?",
  "faqVoorzetplintA10": "Saw with the decorative face up and use a fine-toothed blade. Always dry-fit the corner before fixing it permanently.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ1": "Do I need to level the staircase?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA1": "Only if needed.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ2": "How much material do I need?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA2": "Count the steps and measure the width, depth, and height. Allow for cutting waste.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ3": "Which adhesive should I use?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA3": "An adhesive suitable for laminate.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ4": "Do I need to wait before using the stairs?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA4": "Follow the adhesive manufacturer.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ5": "How do I finish the edges?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA5": "With stair nosings and profiles.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ6": "What if a step is damaged?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA6": "Repair or replace the step.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ7": "How do I maintain the staircase?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA7": "Follow the maintenance advice.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ8": "What safety precautions are required?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA8": "Stable access, ventilation, and personal protection.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ9": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA9": "Measuring, preparation, adhesive, and finishing.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA10": "Finish one step first as a reference.",
  "faqTrapRenoverenQ11": "How do I finish edges and corners?",
  "faqTrapRenoverenA11": "Use stair nosings and profiles suitable for the chosen system. Follow the manufacturer's instructions for a neat finish.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ1": "What is the correct stud spacing?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA1": "Follow the selected wall system and the manufacturer's instructions. A spacing of 600 mm is common in many Metal Stud systems, but it is not universally correct for every board, wall, or load.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ2": "Do I need insulation?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA2": "Insulation depends on the purpose of the wall, such as thermal insulation or sound insulation. Choose a material and thickness suitable for the selected wall system.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ3": "How do I build the frame level?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA3": "Mark out the wall, install the U-profiles according to the system, and check the floor, ceiling, and studs for alignment. Metal Stud U- and C-profiles are normally made from galvanized steel.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ4": "How long should I wait before sealing/jointing?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA4": "There is no general mandatory 24-hour waiting period for the construction. Follow the processing times of the jointing compound, sealant, and other products used.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ5": "How do I finish the joints and screw holes?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA5": "For plasterboard, use a suitable jointing/finishing system, such as joint compound and joint tape where required by the system. Follow the manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ6": "What if the wall or floor is not level?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA6": "Correctly align the frame using suitable, stable fixing and leveling materials. Do not use expanding foam as a general structural filler.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ7": "How do I maintain the wall?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA7": "After proper finishing, maintain the wall according to the selected paint or finishing system. Follow the instructions from the paint and board manufacturer.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ8": "What safety measures are required?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA8": "Use eye, hand, and respiratory protection where necessary. Be careful with sharp profile edges and work from a stable ladder or platform that is suitable for the situation.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ9": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA9": "Common mistakes include: incorrect system selection, incorrect centre-to-centre (h.o.h.) spacing, insufficient fixing, missing reinforcement, and incorrect joint finishing.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ10": "What is the best tip for a strong wall?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA10": "Plan heavy attachment points in advance and install suitable backing/support or system reinforcement according to the wall manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqWandPlaatsenQ11": "How do I hang a heavy object on this wall?",
  "faqWandPlaatsenA11": "Use special plasterboard screws or plugs for light objects. For heavy objects, a reinforced wooden block must be placed behind the plasterboard during construction of the wall.",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerQ1": "Do I need to level, prime, and degrease?",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerA1": "Yes. Check the flatness and load-bearing capacity of the substrate and level it where necessary. Use primer if required by the adhesive system or substrate. Degrease a greasy or contaminated surface, such as a kitchen floor, with a suitable degreaser/cleaner.",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerQ2": "How do I calculate cutting waste and the number of tiles?",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerA2": "Calculate the surface area and divide it by the area of one tile. As a guideline, allow approximately 10% cutting waste. For patterned or diagonal installation, allow approximately 15% or more.",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerQ3": "How do I achieve good adhesion with tile adhesive?",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerA3": "Use the correct notched trowel and adhesive for the tile and substrate. Apply the adhesive to a clean, dust-free surface. For large tiles, use back-buttering according to the adhesive manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerQ4": "How long should I wait before grouting?",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerA4": "Often at least 24 hours. Large tiles, low temperatures, or low-absorption substrates may require more time. Always follow the product data sheet.",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerQ5": "How do I prevent differences in tile height?",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerA5": "Use tile wedges or a tile levelling system. Regularly check with a spirit level and straightedge.",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerQ6": "What should I do with a loose or broken tile?",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerA6": "Carefully remove the tile. Remove loose adhesive and dust from the substrate. Install a suitable replacement tile using appropriate adhesive.",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerQ7": "How do I clean after grouting?",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerA7": "Remove grout residue at the correct time using a suitable sponge and clean water before the grout has completely hardened.",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerQ8": "How do I maintain a tiled floor?",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerA8": "Use a pH-neutral cleaning product suitable for the tile. Avoid abrasive cleaning products.",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerQ9": "What safety measures are required when cutting?",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerA9": "Where possible, use wet cutting or source extraction with a construction vacuum cleaner to reduce silica dust. Wear suitable eye, hearing, and respiratory protection.\n\n⚠️ If in doubt, consult a professional. Always check the instructions for the material being used and the current Dutch regulations.",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerQ10": "How long does it take?",
  "faqVloertegelsBadkamerA10": "Professional: 0.50 hours per m². DIY: 1.20 hours per m².",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenQ1": "Do I need to level, prime, and degrease?",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenA1": "Check that the wall is flat, clean, dry, load-bearing, and free from grease. Level it where necessary and use primer if required by the substrate or adhesive system. Degrease a greasy or contaminated surface, such as a kitchen wall, with a suitable degreaser/cleaner.",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenQ2": "How do I calculate the number of tiles and cutting waste?",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenA2": "Calculate the wall area and divide it by the area of one tile. As a guideline, allow approximately 10% extra. For patterned or diagonal installation, allow approximately 15% or more.",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenQ3": "How do I prevent tiles from coming loose?",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenA3": "Use the correct notched trowel and adhesive for the tile and substrate. Do not apply too much adhesive at once and press the tiles firmly into place. Back-buttering may be required for large tiles.",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenQ4": "How long should I wait before grouting?",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenA4": "Often at least 24 hours. Large tiles or unfavourable conditions may require longer. Follow the product data sheet.",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenQ5": "How do I cut tiles without breaking them?",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenA5": "Use a tile cutter for straight cuts. Use an angle grinder with a diamond blade for cut-outs. Work slowly and support the tile properly.",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenQ6": "What if the wall is not level?",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenA6": "Repair or level the substrate where necessary before tiling. Do not try to compensate indefinitely by applying extra adhesive.",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenQ7": "How do I deal with angled walls?",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenA7": "Measure the angle with a sliding bevel and cut the tiles to size. Start from a square reference line and regularly check with a spirit level.",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenQ8": "How do I clean after grouting?",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenA8": "Use a soft sponge and clean water at the correct time, before the grout has fully hardened. Change the water regularly.",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenQ9": "How do I maintain wall tiles?",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenA9": "Use a damp cloth and mild cleaning product. Avoid abrasive products.\n\n⚠️ If in doubt, consult a professional. Always check the instructions for the material being used and the current Dutch regulations.",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenQ10": "How long does it take?",
  "faqWandtegelsPlaatsenA10": "Professional: 0.40 hours per m². DIY: 1.00 hour per m².",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ1": "How do I remove an old tile without damaging the surrounding tiles?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA1": "If necessary, first cut through the grout around the tile. Carefully drill several holes in the centre of the tile and break it out piece by piece using a chisel.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ2": "Which adhesive should I use for one tile?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA2": "Use a tile adhesive or repair mortar suitable for the tile, substrate, and application. Only use fast-drying products according to the product data sheet.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ3": "How do I make sure the new tile sits level?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA3": "Remove old adhesive without damaging the substrate. Check the flatness and use the correct notched trowel and amount of adhesive. Check with a straightedge and spirit level.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ4": "How long should I wait before grouting?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA4": "This depends on the adhesive. Only grout after the drying and curing time specified on the product data sheet. For fast-drying adhesive, follow the explicit product instructions.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ5": "How do I make the repaired grout joint blend in?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA5": "Use the same grout or demonstrably compatible grout in the same colour. Fill the joint completely and finish it according to the manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ6": "What if the substrate is damaged?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA6": "Repair the substrate first with a suitable repair or levelling compound. Allow it to cure sufficiently.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ7": "How do I prevent adhesive residue?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA7": "Remove excess adhesive immediately using the method specified by the manufacturer, without damaging the tile or grout.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ8": "How do I maintain the repaired tile?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA8": "Maintain it in the same way as the rest of the floor or wall, using a suitable cleaning product.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ9": "What safety measures are required?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA9": "Control dust during drilling and grinding using source extraction with a construction vacuum cleaner or wet processing. Wear appropriate eye and respiratory protection.\n\n⚠️ If in doubt, consult a professional. Always check the instructions for the material being used and the current Dutch regulations.",
  "faqTegelReparerenQ10": "How long does it take?",
  "faqTegelReparerenA10": "Professional: 0.50 hours per tile. DIY: 1.25 hours per tile.",
  "faqVoegenQ1": "Do the joints need to be clean and dry?",
  "faqVoegenA1": "Yes. Remove adhesive residue and dirt from the joints. The joints must meet the drying and processing requirements of the selected grout.",
  "faqVoegenQ2": "Which grout should I use indoors?",
  "faqVoegenA2": "Choose based on the tile type, joint width, and application. Cement-based or epoxy grout may be suitable indoors. Follow the product data sheet.",
  "faqVoegenQ3": "How do I mix grout?",
  "faqVoegenA3": "Follow the exact mixing ratio and processing time stated on the packaging. Use clean water and a suitable mixer.",
  "faqVoegenQ4": "How long should grout cure before the surface can be used?",
  "faqVoegenA4": "This varies depending on the product, temperature, and application. Follow the waiting time for use and complete curing stated on the product data sheet.",
  "faqVoegenQ5": "How do I prevent grout from drying out or cracking?",
  "faqVoegenA5": "Work in small sections. Avoid drafts, direct sunlight, and excessively rapid drying. Only moisten the grout if the manufacturer specifically instructs you to do so.",
  "faqVoegenQ6": "What should I do about uneven grout joints?",
  "faqVoegenA6": "Finish the grout within the permitted processing time using the correct tools. After curing, repairs may remain visible. Follow the product instructions for repair.",
  "faqVoegenQ7": "How do I clean tiles without damaging the grout?",
  "faqVoegenA7": "Clean at the correct time with a suitable sponge and a limited amount of clean water. Do not wait until a hard cement haze has formed.",
  "faqVoegenQ8": "How do I maintain grout?",
  "faqVoegenA8": "Clean regularly with a product suitable for the type of grout. Impregnation may be appropriate for some cement-based grouts.",
  "faqVoegenQ9": "What safety measures are required?",
  "faqVoegenA9": "Wear suitable gloves and eye protection. Control dust when mixing powdered products and ensure adequate ventilation.\n\n⚠️ If in doubt, consult a professional. Always check the instructions for the material being used and the current Dutch regulations.",
  "faqVoegenQ10": "How long does it take?",
  "faqVoegenA10": "Professional: 0.10 hours per m². DIY: 0.25 hours per m².",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ1": "Do I need to degrease and dry the surface?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA1": "Yes. Clean the substrate and make sure it is free from grease and dust. Allow it to dry unless the product specifically states otherwise.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ2": "Which sealant should I use?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA2": "Silicone sealant is suitable for many sanitary and wet-area joints. Acrylic sealant is often used for paintable joints indoors.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ3": "How do I remove old sealant?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA3": "Carefully cut or scrape away the old sealant using a sealant scraper. If necessary, use a suitable sealant remover. Then clean the surface according to the instructions for the new sealant product.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ4": "How do I apply sealant evenly?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA4": "Cut the nozzle diagonally to the desired joint width. Apply the sealant evenly. Smooth it with a rubber sealant finishing tool or suitable sealant spatula. Use a spray bottle with a finishing agent approved by the manufacturer.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ5": "How long should I wait before the sealant can be exposed to load?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA5": "This depends on the sealant type, joint dimensions, and environmental conditions. Follow the skin-formation, curing, and load-bearing times stated on the packaging.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ6": "How do I smooth sealant?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA6": "Use a rubber sealant finishing tool or suitable sealant spatula together with a finishing agent approved by the manufacturer. Do not automatically use dishwashing liquid.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ7": "What if the sealant does not adhere?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA7": "Remove the sealant. Check whether the substrate is suitable. Clean and dry the surface again. If necessary, use the primer specified by the manufacturer.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ8": "How do I maintain sealant joints?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA8": "Regularly check for detachment, cracks, mould, and discolouration. Repair or replace the sealant joint when the seal is no longer effective.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ9": "What safety measures are required?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA9": "Ensure sufficient ventilation. Wear suitable gloves. Avoid contact with skin and eyes.\n\n⚠️ If in doubt, consult a professional. Always check the instructions for the material being used and the current Dutch regulations.",
  "faqKitAanbrengenQ10": "How long does it take?",
  "faqKitAanbrengenA10": "Professional: 0.05 hours per metre. DIY: 0.125 hours per metre.",
  "faqKraanVervangenQ1": "Must I shut off the water?",
  "faqKraanVervangenA1": "Yes. Close the relevant shut-off valve or main tap and depressurize the pipe.",
  "faqKraanVervangenQ2": "How do I remove the old tap?",
  "faqKraanVervangenA2": "Use suitable tools and hold fixed connections steady. Do not force stuck or damaged connections.",
  "faqKraanVervangenQ3": "How do I know which tap I need?",
  "faqKraanVervangenA3": "Check whether it's countertop or wall mounting and measure the existing connection and available space.",
  "faqKraanVervangenQ4": "What connection does a standard countertop tap have?",
  "faqKraanVervangenA4": "Many kitchen taps are supplied with flexible connection hoses and a standard connection. Always check the specific product.",
  "faqKraanVervangenQ5": "Must a wooden countertop be sealed?",
  "faqKraanVervangenA5": "With a wooden countertop, sealing around the tap opening may be necessary to prevent moisture penetration. Follow the countertop and sealant specifications.",
  "faqKraanVervangenQ6": "How do I check for leaks?",
  "faqKraanVervangenA6": "Open the water supply gently and check all connections with a dry cloth.",
  "faqKraanVervangenQ7": "What if the tap keeps leaking?",
  "faqKraanVervangenA7": "Shut off the water and check the connection, seal, and mounting method. Do not overtighten connections indefinitely.",
  "faqKraanVervangenQ8": "Can I always replace an old tap with a new one?",
  "faqKraanVervangenA8": "Not without checking. Check connection, hole size, available space, and tap type.",
  "faqKraanVervangenQ9": "Most common mistake?",
  "faqKraanVervangenA9": "Wrong measurements or overtightening a connection.",
  "faqKraanVervangenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqKraanVervangenA10": "Take a photo before dismantling and compare all connections with the new tap.",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenQ1": "Which tool should I use first?",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenA1": "Start with a plunger for a light blockage.",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenQ2": "When do I use a drain snake?",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenA2": "When the plunger gives insufficient results and the blockage is accessible.",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenQ3": "Must I use chemical drain cleaner?",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenA3": "Not as standard. Mechanical unblocking is preferred. Use a chemical agent only when suitable for the application and follow the label exactly.",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenQ4": "May I mix different drain cleaners?",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenA4": "No. Never mix different chemical agents.",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenQ5": "Can I clean the siphon?",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenA5": "With an accessible siphon, it can be dismantled and cleaned, provided this is safe and appropriate.",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenQ6": "What if the blockage returns?",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenA6": "Have the cause investigated instead of repeatedly using agents.",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenQ7": "When to call a professional?",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenA7": "For a deep, stubborn, or recurring blockage or suspected pipe damage.",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenQ8": "What protection?",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenA8": "Gloves and eye protection. Extra caution when chemicals have been used previously.",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenQ9": "What is a common mistake?",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenA9": "Forcing too hard or mixing chemical agents.",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqLeidingOntstoppenA10": "Start mechanically and investigate recurring blockages.",
  "faqToiletInstallerenQ1": "Must the existing drain fit?",
  "faqToiletInstallerenA1": "Yes. Check position, size, type, and connection options before purchase.",
  "faqToiletInstallerenQ2": "Standard or raised toilet?",
  "faqToiletInstallerenA2": "A standard toilet is the starting point. A raised toilet has a higher seat height; the exact height varies by model.",
  "faqToiletInstallerenQ3": "Can every toilet fit every drain?",
  "faqToiletInstallerenA3": "No. Check the connection dimensions and position.",
  "faqToiletInstallerenQ4": "How do I fix the toilet?",
  "faqToiletInstallerenA4": "Use the manufacturer's fixing and ensure the toilet is stable and level.",
  "faqToiletInstallerenQ5": "What is sanitary sealant for?",
  "faqToiletInstallerenA5": "For sealing where required by the system. Sealant is not a replacement for mechanical fixing.",
  "faqToiletInstallerenQ6": "How do I check the connection?",
  "faqToiletInstallerenA6": "After installation, flush gently and check all connections for leaks.",
  "faqToiletInstallerenQ7": "What if there's a leaking connection?",
  "faqToiletInstallerenA7": "Shut off the water and check the relevant connection again.",
  "faqToiletInstallerenQ8": "What protection?",
  "faqToiletInstallerenA8": "Gloves, safe lifting, and accounting for residual water.",
  "faqToiletInstallerenQ9": "Most common mistakes?",
  "faqToiletInstallerenA9": "Wrong drain size, wrong connection, or insufficient fixing.",
  "faqToiletInstallerenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqToiletInstallerenA10": "Measure all connection dimensions first before buying the toilet.",
  "faqCvBijvullenQ1": "When must I refill?",
  "faqCvBijvullenA1": "When the pressure is too low according to the appliance manual.",
  "faqCvBijvullenQ2": "What value should I maintain?",
  "faqCvBijvullenA2": "The specified value of the specific appliance is leading.",
  "faqCvBijvullenQ3": "Must the installation be cooled?",
  "faqCvBijvullenA3": "Follow the appliance manual for this.",
  "faqCvBijvullenQ4": "How do I refill?",
  "faqCvBijvullenA4": "Use only the prescribed refill procedure.",
  "faqCvBijvullenQ5": "Must I bleed afterwards?",
  "faqCvBijvullenA5": "Only when this is necessary according to the manual or the situation.",
  "faqCvBijvullenQ6": "What if the pressure keeps dropping?",
  "faqCvBijvullenA6": "Do not keep refilling. Recurring pressure loss may indicate a problem or leak.",
  "faqCvBijvullenQ7": "May I repair the boiler myself?",
  "faqCvBijvullenA7": "No. Work on gas combustion installations must be carried out by a certified company.",
  "faqCvBijvullenQ8": "May I open gas-side components?",
  "faqCvBijvullenA8": "No.",
  "faqCvBijvullenQ9": "What is a common mistake?",
  "faqCvBijvullenA9": "Refilling too much or too fast and ignoring recurring pressure loss.",
  "faqCvBijvullenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqCvBijvullenA10": "Keep the appliance manual and follow the specific refill procedure.",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenQ1": "What material should I use for water or drainage?",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenA1": "Use a system suitable for the application and the chosen water pipe purpose.",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenQ2": "What diameter must I use?",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenA2": "Not automatically 12 or 15 mm. The required diameter depends on application, pipe length, required flow rate, and the chosen system.",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenQ3": "Must hot water pipe be insulated?",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenA3": "Where heat loss must be limited, pipe insulation can be applied according to installation requirements.",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenQ4": "How much pipe do I need?",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenA4": "Measure the full route and account for bends, connections, and cutting waste.",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenQ5": "How do I connect copper pipe?",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenA5": "Use a suitable connection system and follow the manufacturer and installation specifications.",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenQ6": "Can I use a pipe bender?",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenA6": "Yes, when suitable for the chosen pipe and application.",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenQ7": "How do I fix pipes?",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenA7": "Use suitable fixing according to the chosen system, material, diameter, and temperature load.",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenQ8": "May I just chase a wall?",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenA8": "No. Check beforehand for existing pipes, cables, and building constraints.",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenQ9": "How do I test the pipe?",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenA9": "Test the installation according to the specifications of the chosen system before it is finally covered.",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqLeidingAanleggenA10": "Make a pipe plan beforehand with route, connection points, shut-off points, and material choice. ⚠️ For drinking water installations, suitable materials and applicable Dutch requirements must be followed, including NEN 1006 and the Water worksheets.",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenQ1": "Should I use an earthed or unearthed socket?",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenA1": "Earthed sockets are required for appliances with an earthed plug (washing machines, dishwashers, microwaves) and are mandatory in all wet areas such as bathrooms, kitchens, utility rooms, garages, and outdoors. An unearthed socket is only for double-insulated appliances and is no longer used in wet areas for new installations. When in doubt, or in a wet area, always choose an earthed socket.",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenQ2": "Do I need to switch off the electricity?",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenA2": "Yes. Switch off the circuit and use a two-pole voltage tester to confirm that the installation is de-energized. Never work on a live installation.",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenQ3": "How many flush-mounted boxes do I need?",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenA3": "One flush-mounted box per socket. For a double socket, use a double box or two single boxes.",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenQ4": "How do I connect the socket?",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenA4": "Connect the live, neutral, and protective earth conductors according to the product's wiring diagram and the applicable installation requirements. The wiring is usually already present in the wall. Use wire connectors or a terminal block. In wet areas, a 30 mA residual-current device (RCD) is mandatory.",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenQ5": "How long does it take?",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenA5": "Professional: 0.75 hours per socket. DIY: 1.9 hours per socket. Flush-mounted installation can take longer due to chiselling, drilling, and repair work.",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenQ6": "What if the socket does not work?",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenA6": "Switch off the power and check the connection, wiring, and protection. If in doubt, do not switch it back on. Consult a qualified person.",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenQ7": "What safety measures are required?",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenA7": "Never work on a live installation. Use suitable measuring equipment. Wear appropriate protective equipment. Use a safe working method. Use a cable/pipe detector before drilling.",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenQ8": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenA8": "Incorrect connection or component, forgetting the protective earth, failing to verify the circuit is de-energized, insufficient fixing, incorrect flush-mounted box, and installing an unearthed socket in a wet area where earthing is mandatory.",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenQ9": "Can I do this myself?",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenA9": "Yes, provided you switch off the electricity, use a voltage tester, and follow the connection instructions. If unsure about earthing or modifying the distribution board, consult a qualified electrician.",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqStopcontactPlaatsenA10": "If safe to do so, take a photo of the original situation before disconnecting anything. Never rely solely on the photograph — verify wiring with measuring equipment and the product wiring diagram. In wet areas, always use an earthed socket with an RCD. Consult a professional if in doubt. Always check current Dutch regulations, applicable installation requirements, and the technical product data sheet.",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenQ1": "What type of lamp or light fixture should I choose?",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenA1": "For indoor areas, a ceiling light, pendant light, or surface-mounted fixture is most common. In wet areas such as bathrooms and kitchens, use a moisture-resistant fixture with an appropriate IP rating, for example IP44 or higher.",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenQ2": "Do I need to switch off the electricity?",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenA2": "Yes. Switch off the circuit and use a two-pole voltage tester to confirm the installation is de-energized. Never work on a live installation.",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenQ3": "How many light points do I need?",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenA3": "This depends on the room, its function, and the desired light level. There is no universal number.",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenQ4": "How do I install the light fixture?",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenA4": "Install the mounting bracket according to the manufacturer's instructions. Connect the wiring using the wiring diagram and install the fixture. The wiring is usually already present in the ceiling. Use a terminal block or wire connectors. Only connect protective earth when required by the fixture or installation system.",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenQ5": "How long does it take?",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenA5": "Professional: 0.50 hours per light point. DIY: 1.25 hours per light point. Wet areas may require additional time for suitable sealing and installation.",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenQ6": "What if the light does not turn on?",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenA6": "First switch off the electricity. Check the connection, switch, and protection using a safe testing procedure. If in doubt, consult an electrician.",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenQ7": "What safety measures are required?",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenA7": "Never work on a live installation. Use a stable ladder and suitable eye and hand protection. Use a cable/pipe detector before drilling.",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenQ8": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenA8": "Incorrect fixture for a wet area, poor electrical connection, incorrect installation, and incorrect connection of the protective earth where required.",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenQ9": "What should I consider in wet areas?",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenA9": "Use a fixture with an appropriate IP rating, at least IP44 for bathrooms and kitchens. Ensure proper sealing and connect the protective earth correctly when required by the system.",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqVerlichtingPlaatsenA10": "Choose a colour temperature and dimming capability appropriate for the room. Before purchasing, check that the dimmer, lamp, and fixture are compatible. In wet areas, always use an appropriate IP rating. Consult a professional if in doubt. Always check current Dutch regulations, applicable installation requirements, and the technical product data sheet.",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenQ1": "Which cable or wire should I choose?",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenA1": "The cable type and conductor cross-section depend on the application, installation method, protection, electrical load, and applicable installation requirements. VD wire is used in an appropriate conduit/system. Cable types such as XMVK have their own application requirements.",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenQ2": "Do I need to switch off the electricity?",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenA2": "Yes. Switch off the circuit and use suitable measuring equipment to verify the working area is de-energized.",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenQ3": "How many metres do I need?",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenA3": "Measure the complete route and allow extra length for bends and connection points. 10% extra is a practical guideline, although complex routes may require more.",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenQ4": "How do I install the cable?",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenA4": "Mark the route and check for existing cables and pipes with a cable/pipe detector. Use suitable equipment and dust extraction for chasing and drilling. The chase depth and installation method must suit the cable/conduit system, wall construction, and applicable requirements. Install the conduit or cable and pull the wires. Cut conduit to length using a hacksaw.",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenQ5": "How long does it take?",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenA5": "Professional: 0.10 hours per metre. DIY: 0.25 hours per metre. Preparation, routing, wall type, and finishing can affect total time.",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenQ6": "What if a cable is damaged?",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenA6": "Switch off the electricity. Damaged cables must not simply be covered up. Repair them using an appropriate and standards-compliant method.",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenQ7": "What safety measures are required?",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenA7": "Work with the power switched off. Check for existing cables and structural elements before starting. Wear appropriate protective equipment. Avoid unsafe deep or long chases in load-bearing walls — have structural concerns assessed by a qualified professional.",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenQ8": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenA8": "Incorrect cable or conductor size, bends that are too sharp, poor fixing, insufficient pulling provision, hitting existing cables, and failing to check the wall before chasing.",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenQ9": "Can I chase a load-bearing wall?",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenA9": "No. Deep or long horizontal chases in load-bearing walls can reduce structural strength. Always have structural questions assessed by a qualified professional.",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqStroomleidingLeggenA10": "Plan the route carefully and document cables before closing the chases. For future modifications, only use provisions designed for the intended application. Consult a professional if in doubt. Always check current Dutch regulations, applicable installation requirements, and the technical product data sheet.",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenQ1": "Which type of cable trunking should I use?",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenA1": "Self-adhesive trunking is for lightweight cables on a smooth, clean surface. Screw-mounted trunking is for heavier cables, rougher surfaces, or outdoor use. A common indoor size is 20 × 12 mm to 25 × 16 mm.",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenQ2": "Do I need to prepare the surface?",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenA2": "Yes. Clean the surface and make sure it is grease-free and dry when required by the manufacturer. Self-adhesive systems require particular attention to surface preparation.",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenQ3": "How many metres do I need?",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenA3": "Measure the route and allow extra material for corners, connections, and cutting waste. 10% extra is a practical guideline.",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenQ4": "How do I install the cable trunking?",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenA4": "Follow the installation instructions for the selected system. Cut it straight to size using a hacksaw. For corners, use suitable accessories or a neat mitre joint. Use a stable ladder when working at height.",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenQ5": "How long does it take?",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenA5": "Professional: 0.07 hours per metre. DIY: 0.17 hours per metre. Drilling into hard surfaces can require additional time.",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenQ6": "What if the cable trunking comes loose?",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenA6": "Remove the loose material, clean the surface, and repair it according to the manufacturer's recommended fixing method. For heavier loads, use suitable mechanical fixing.",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenQ7": "What safety measures are required?",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenA7": "Wear gloves and eye protection. Watch out for sharp edges. Use a cable/pipe detector before drilling.",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenQ8": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenA8": "Not cleaning the surface, incorrect fixing method for the surface, not marking the route straight, incorrect size, and insufficient fixing points.",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenQ9": "Can I use cable trunking outdoors?",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenA9": "Only if the cable trunking is specifically suitable for outdoor use. Preferably use screw mounting rather than self-adhesive mounting.",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqKabelgootMonterenA10": "For corners, use suitable accessories or a neat mitre joint. Install the cable trunking straight and tightly. Consult a professional if in doubt. Always check current Dutch regulations, applicable installation requirements, and the technical product data sheet.",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenQ1": "Which type of smoke detector should I use?",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenA1": "An optical smoke detector is a common choice for many residential rooms. In a kitchen, a heat detector may be more suitable. Choose a product that meets the relevant requirements.",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenQ2": "Where should I install the detector?",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenA2": "The Dutch government recommends installing smoke detectors on the ceiling, preferably in the hallway, at least 50 cm from a wall or corner and at least 30 cm from a light fixture. Do not place the detector directly next to ventilation openings or heating. Always follow the manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenQ3": "How many detectors do I need?",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenA3": "In the Netherlands, a smoke detector is mandatory on every residential floor. The exact number and best locations depend on the property. Follow current legal requirements and the manufacturer's instructions.",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenQ4": "How do I install the detector?",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenA4": "Install the mounting bracket according to the manufacturer's instructions. Attach the detector and test it after installation. Use appropriate wall plugs and screws when mechanical fixing is required.",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenQ5": "How long does it take?",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenA5": "Professional: 0.25 hours per detector. DIY: 0.60 hours per detector. Drilling, accessibility, and ceiling type can require additional time.",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenQ6": "What should I do if there are false alarms?",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenA6": "First determine the cause. Clean dust according to the manufacturer's instructions and check the detector's location, ventilation, and moisture conditions. Only move the detector if the new location meets the applicable requirements.",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenQ7": "What safety measures are required?",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenA7": "Use a stable ladder and work safely. Test the detector after installation and follow the manufacturer's instructions. Use a cable/pipe detector before drilling into the ceiling.",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenQ8": "What are the most common mistakes?",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenA8": "Incorrect positioning, not testing the detector, empty or missing battery, dirt/contamination, and not replacing the detector when the manufacturer specifies the end of its service life.",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenQ9": "How do I test the detector?",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenA9": "Test it regularly using the test button according to the manufacturer's instructions. Keep the detector free of dust and follow the maintenance and replacement instructions for the specific model.",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenQ10": "Best tip?",
  "faqRookmelderPlaatsenA10": "Use the test button regularly and replace the battery or complete detector according to the manufacturer's instructions. Take the legal positioning requirements into account. Consult a professional if in doubt. Always check current Dutch regulations, applicable installation requirements, and the technical product data sheet.",
